    "created_at": "2018-03-28T16:25:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-03-28T16:25:28Z",
    "body": "For your first point, it has always been the case that when flagalloc needs to \"spill\" and \"restore\" flags, it leaves the old flag computation behind.  It shouldn't be too hard to eliminate those comparisons.  It wouldn't even require a full deadcode pass, because we know the args of the comparison are still used.\r\n\r\nFor your second point, this is working as expected, sort of.  CMPQmem are generated, but they need to be undone during flagalloc.  The problem is that we first schedule the CMPQmem, then a CMPQ for the bounds check.  Because the bounds check clobbers flags, flagalloc must move the flag computation later, but it doesn't think it is safe to move the load later (it is safe, in this case, but it isn't in general).\r\nI don't know whether we should fix this by making the scheduling smarter (moving the bounds checks earlier, maybe?) or teaching flagalloc to know when it is ok to move the load later.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
